course: dictionary
lesson: 14692
title: Prevalence: how common something is
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prevalence is a noun. It tells you how common or widespread something is, in a place or group, at a certain time. «The prevalence of colds is high in winter» means colds are very common in winter.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. Words like high, low, rising or falling tell you about prevalence. «The prevalence of mobile phones in our school is high» means most children in the school have one.
explain: Prevalence is often shown as a percentage or a fraction of a group. If 20 children out of 100 have a pet, the prevalence is 20 per cent, which is one fifth.
fact: Prevalence means how common or widespread something is.
fact: Prevalence is used in science, health and the news.
fact: In statistics, prevalence is often written as a percentage or a fraction of a group.
fact: Prevalence is uncountable, so you say «the prevalence», not «a prevalence».
fact: A good check is to swap in the words «how common» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The prevalence of asthma in children is quite high in our town.» What does prevalence mean here? | How big the children's lungs are | *How common asthma is among children | How much medicine the town has | The name of the town's hospital
exercise: Nina reads: «In a class of 25, 5 children wear glasses. The prevalence is 5 out of 25.» What fraction is the prevalence? | One tenth | Two fifths | *One fifth | One half
exercise: Which sentence uses prevalence correctly? | «She ran a prevalence around the park.» | «I ate a big prevalence for lunch.» | «The prevalence was a blue bike.» | *«The prevalence of colds rises in winter.»
test: «Mobile phones have a high prevalence in our school.» What does this mean? | Phones are not allowed in school | *Most children in the school have one | The school sells phones | Mobile phones are very heavy
test: Ravi says prevalence is the same as the number of people in a place. Is he right? | No, it is only used for animals | *No, it is about how common something is among those people | Yes, it means the same as a crowd | Yes, it counts every person
exam: In a survey of 200 pupils, 50 pupils have a pet. What is the prevalence of pets as a percentage? | *25 per cent | 50 per cent | 10 per cent | 75 per cent
